{"causes": ["Its strategic geographical location at the confluence of the Jordan River valley and the Jezreel Valley fostered continuous settlement and trade.", "The fertile agricultural lands and abundant perennial water sources from the Harod Stream supported a large and stable population.", "Its position on major ancient trade routes facilitated economic prosperity and cultural exchange between Egypt, Syria, and Mesopotamia."], "effects": ["The accumulation of numerous archaeological strata, or 'tells,' provides an unparalleled stratigraphic record of regional human history.", "Its development into a major administrative and cultural center, particularly as the Roman city of Scythopolis, influenced regional urban planning.", "The preservation of monumental Roman and Byzantine architecture, including a theater and bathhouses, offers insights into classical urban life."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "new-kingdom-egypt", "sourceName": "New Kingdom of Egypt", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "beit-shean", "targetName": "Beit She'an", "context": "Beit She'an served as an important administrative center and garrison town for the New Kingdom of Egypt during the Late Bronze Age, from the 15th to the 12th century BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "roman-empire", "sourceName": "Roman Empire", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "beit-shean", "targetName": "Beit She'an", "context": "The city of Beit She'an, known as Scythopolis, flourished as a prominent city of the Decapolis within the Roman province of Syria Palaestina from the 1st century BCE to the 4th century CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "earthquake-of-749-ce", "sourceName": "Earthquake of 749 CE", "verb": "TRANSFORMS", "targetSlug": "beit-shean", "targetName": "Beit She'an", "context": "The devastating Galilee earthquake of 749 CE largely destroyed the thriving Byzantine city of Scythopolis, leading to its significant decline and eventual abandonment of many monumental structures."}, {"sourceSlug": "hellenistic-period", "sourceName": "Hellenistic Period", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "beit-shean", "targetName": "Beit She'an", "context": "During the Hellenistic period, Beit She'an was refounded as Scythopolis, adopting Greek urban planning and cultural elements that laid the groundwork for its later Roman prosperity."}, {"sourceSlug": "byzantine-empire", "sourceName": "Byzantine Empire", "verb": "CONTAINS", "targetSlug": "beit-shean", "targetName": "Beit She'an", "context": "Beit She'an, as Scythopolis, was a major Christian center within the Byzantine Empire from the 4th to the 7th century CE, marked by extensive church construction and a large population."}], "places": [{"name": "Beit She'an, Israel", "role": "Primary archaeological site"}, {"name": "Jordan Valley, Israel", "role": "Geographic location"}, {"name": "Scythopolis, Roman Empire", "role": "Major Roman city"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Beit She'an", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "beit-shean"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q152368", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beit_She%27an"], "tags": ["Israel", "city", "Asia", "Cities", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Beit She'an is remembered as a landmark place of the Prehistoric era, referenced in 52 Wikipedia language editions. Key lasting impacts include: Served as a center of human activity and development. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Beit She'an's continuous occupation over millennia provides an unparalleled archaeological record of cultural succession in the Levant, from Prehistory through the Islamic Golden Age, culminating in the monumental Roman city of Scythopolis. Its ruins offer critical insights into ancient urbanism, imperial administration, and the impact of natural disasters on civilization.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-07-19T22:23:10Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29705983665", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Beit She'an, also known as Beisan, or Beth-shean, is a city in the Northern District of Israel. The town lies at the Beit She'an Valley about 120 m below sea level. In 2022, 98% of the population was Jewish and 2% was counted as other.", "newValue": "Beit She'an, known through millennia as Beisan and later as Scythopolis, stands as an archaeological palimpsest in the Northern District of Israel, situated strategically in the fertile Jordan Valley, approximately 120 meters below sea level. Its continuous occupation spans over 7,000 years, from the Neolithic period through the Ottoman era, making it one of the most significant tells in the Levant for understanding successive layers of civilization. This enduring settlement, positioned at the nexus of ancient trade routes connecting Egypt, Syria, and Mesopotamia, served as a crucial administrative, economic, and cultural hub for various empires and peoples.

The city reached its zenith during the Roman and Byzantine periods, when it was known as Scythopolis, the only city of the Decapolis west of the Jordan River. Under Roman rule, Scythopolis flourished, boasting impressive public structures including a grand theater seating 7,000 spectators, a hippodrome, bathhouses, colonnaded streets, and a monumental nymphaeum. This prosperity continued into the Byzantine era, marked by the construction of numerous churches and monasteries, alongside a thriving Jewish community. However, its flourishing came to an abrupt end with the devastating Galilee earthquake of 749 CE, which razed much of the city, leading to its gradual decline.

Despite the catastrophic earthquake, Beit She'an maintained a reduced settlement under early Islamic rule, with its strategic location ensuring some continued significance. The ruins of Scythopolis became a quarry for later construction, yet the sheer scale and preservation of its Roman and Byzantine remains continue to draw scholars and tourists. The site offers an unparalleled opportunity to trace urban development, architectural styles, and cultural interactions across millennia, from its earliest Canaanite temples to its sophisticated Byzantine mosaics."}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-19T22:23:10Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29705983665", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": ["Human settlement and community formation"], "newValue": ["Its strategic geographical location at the confluence of the Jordan River valley and the Jezreel Valley fostered continuous settlement and trade.", "The fertile agricultural lands and abundant perennial water sources from the Harod Stream supported a large and stable population.", "Its position on major ancient trade routes facilitated economic prosperity and cultural exchange between Egypt, Syria, and Mesopotamia."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-19T22:23:10Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29705983665", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": ["Served as a center of human activity and development"], "newValue": ["The accumulation of numerous archaeological strata, or 'tells,' provides an unparalleled stratigraphic record of regional human history.", "Its development into a major administrative and cultural center, particularly as the Roman city of Scythopolis, influenced regional urban planning.", "The preservation of monumental Roman and Byzantine architecture, including a theater and bathhouses, offers insights into classical urban life."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-07-19T22:23:10Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#29705983665", "field": "detailsJson.relationships", "oldValue": [{"sourceSlug": "beit-shean",